1
00:00:00,210 --> 00:00:01,560
Hello, everybody.

2
00:00:01,560 --> 00:00:02,700
This is Lilly.

3
00:00:02,700 --> 00:00:11,040
And in today's lecture, we are going to talk about software testing and graphic user interface.

4
00:00:11,340 --> 00:00:20,520
So we believe this lecture is going to be useful for QA specialists or those who want to know more about

5
00:00:20,520 --> 00:00:23,430
what QA specialists do.

6
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:25,290
So let's start.

7
00:00:26,490 --> 00:00:27,450
All right.

8
00:00:27,750 --> 00:00:33,720
And you probably know it, but we will still start with a definition.

9
00:00:33,720 --> 00:00:42,360
So software testing is a process of checking if software product meets requirements and is free of defects.

10
00:00:42,720 --> 00:00:46,320
So basically a process to check if

11
00:00:47,370 --> 00:00:49,920
any software works well.

12
00:00:50,790 --> 00:00:59,910
And if you work in software testing, you probably know those abbreviations.

13
00:00:59,910 --> 00:01:02,960
And if you don't, we will look at them now.

14
00:01:02,970 --> 00:01:11,550
So we often hear QA and QC, I find it that sometimes, even if people work in QA, they may not

15
00:01:11,550 --> 00:01:13,650
be sure what it means, right.

16
00:01:13,650 --> 00:01:14,340
So.

17
00:01:14,620 --> 00:01:23,100
QA stands for Quality Assurance and QC stands for Quality Control.

18
00:01:24,090 --> 00:01:30,240
And here on this graph, you may see how those things are related.

19
00:01:31,340 --> 00:01:38,710
So for example, quality control is a part is a part of quality assurance.

20
00:01:38,720 --> 00:01:41,540
They are all part of quality management.

21
00:01:42,370 --> 00:01:46,510
And here we can see the definitions, right.

22
00:01:46,750 --> 00:01:53,830
So those definitions are taken from a trustful source, actually.

23
00:01:54,280 --> 00:01:56,530
And here is the link.

24
00:01:58,340 --> 00:02:01,930
Well, they are taken from

25
00:02:02,180 --> 00:02:04,520
ISTQB, right.

26
00:02:04,520 --> 00:02:08,810
So this is an international software testing qualifications board.

27
00:02:09,139 --> 00:02:12,870
Again, if you work in QA, you probably know what it is.

28
00:02:12,890 --> 00:02:21,740
So this is an organization where you can get your certification, right, as a specialist, and there is

29
00:02:21,740 --> 00:02:22,940
a glossary there.

30
00:02:23,450 --> 00:02:29,870
So if you want, you can go there and check the meaning of some terms here with the link, you can see

31
00:02:30,830 --> 00:02:35,600
the glossary is like a dictionary of some terms.

32
00:02:36,410 --> 00:02:46,340
But of course those definitions are quite hard, hard to understand, very technical, and it can be

33
00:02:46,340 --> 00:02:47,580
a problem, right?

34
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:53,270
Especially if your level of English is not very good.

35
00:02:53,270 --> 00:02:53,960
Right.

36
00:02:55,010 --> 00:02:57,050
Let's take a look at this one, for example.

37
00:02:57,050 --> 00:03:05,540
So QA, as we said quality assurance - activities focused on providing confidence that quality requirements

38
00:03:05,540 --> 00:03:07,010
will be fulfilled.

39
00:03:08,900 --> 00:03:14,930
So there are definitely easier ways to say this, but that that is what we have here.

40
00:03:15,290 --> 00:03:20,560
And let's look at the words that are used in this definition.

41
00:03:20,570 --> 00:03:26,150
Well, for example, quality, the standard of something, right?

42
00:03:26,450 --> 00:03:31,970
If you don't know what it means, I recommend checking what it means in your own language.

43
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:34,520
This is a pretty important word, quality.

44
00:03:35,150 --> 00:03:43,850
Then we have assurance and this comes from assure, which is a verb, means to tell someone something

45
00:03:43,850 --> 00:03:45,560
will definitely happen.

46
00:03:45,860 --> 00:03:48,290
Or like to make sure, right.

47
00:03:48,290 --> 00:03:50,600
To make sure, to assure someone.

48
00:03:52,400 --> 00:03:58,100
The next one, a pretty important one, is requirement -something that is needed.

49
00:03:58,460 --> 00:03:59,150
So what

50
00:03:59,150 --> 00:04:03,200
this software has to be like, the requirement.

51
00:04:03,560 --> 00:04:07,190
Here we have a fulfill, right?

52
00:04:07,190 --> 00:04:08,990
Fulfill requirements.

53
00:04:09,320 --> 00:04:15,310
But less formal way is to say meet requirements.

54
00:04:15,320 --> 00:04:17,990
And it is used more often actually.

55
00:04:17,990 --> 00:04:18,529
Right.

56
00:04:20,029 --> 00:04:21,019
Okay.

57
00:04:21,260 --> 00:04:23,450
Requirements will be fulfilled.

58
00:04:23,480 --> 00:04:24,980
This is actually Passive.

59
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:30,140
And we are going to have a lecture on passive in this course in grammar.

60
00:04:31,190 --> 00:04:32,150
All right.

61
00:04:32,150 --> 00:04:37,490
Well, as I said, this is a pretty difficult definition.

62
00:04:37,490 --> 00:04:40,940
I mean, like in terms of English, English language.

63
00:04:40,940 --> 00:04:43,940
So there are much easier ways to say it.

64
00:04:43,970 --> 00:04:51,260
For example, you could say quality assurance means activities that help you make sure that software

65
00:04:51,260 --> 00:04:53,430
meets quality requirements.

66
00:04:53,450 --> 00:04:56,600
Instead of saying providing confidence and so on.

67
00:04:56,930 --> 00:05:05,600
But of course, if you want to get that certificate, you have to learn all of that and try to understand

68
00:05:05,600 --> 00:05:06,230
those

69
00:05:07,380 --> 00:05:09,270
complicated definitions.

70
00:05:09,870 --> 00:05:13,710
So this is also a good training for English.

71
00:05:14,990 --> 00:05:15,410
All right.

72
00:05:15,410 --> 00:05:17,290
Now let's look at quality control.

73
00:05:17,300 --> 00:05:23,540
We looked at quality assurance there and then quality control, for example, a set of activities designed

74
00:05:23,540 --> 00:05:27,110
to evaluate the quality of a component or a system.

75
00:05:27,620 --> 00:05:29,390
So we already know quality.

76
00:05:30,320 --> 00:05:35,030
I also wanted you to pay attention to the word evaluate.

77
00:05:35,630 --> 00:05:40,160
So to check how good or useful something is, right.

78
00:05:40,880 --> 00:05:48,320
If you evaluate something, you are able to say, Oh, this is good or not very good, needs more work.

79
00:05:49,780 --> 00:05:52,960
And the next word component.

80
00:05:53,410 --> 00:05:56,860
Please pay attention to pronunciation - compOnent.

81
00:05:57,400 --> 00:05:59,650
A part of something, here

82
00:05:59,650 --> 00:06:02,020
a part of a system, of course.

83
00:06:03,400 --> 00:06:04,540
All right.

84
00:06:06,090 --> 00:06:15,240
And yes, of course, if you want to understand more how those things are related, what is the connection

85
00:06:15,240 --> 00:06:16,140
between them?

86
00:06:16,170 --> 00:06:18,670
Of course, this is not a technical course.

87
00:06:18,690 --> 00:06:20,580
This is a language course.

88
00:06:20,850 --> 00:06:23,940
But at least we have some tips for you.

89
00:06:25,110 --> 00:06:26,900
You can go with that on that site

90
00:06:26,910 --> 00:06:31,710
I mentioned the ISTQB and there are some downloadables.

91
00:06:32,190 --> 00:06:33,360
Here is the link.

92
00:06:33,660 --> 00:06:38,030
So some downloadables are for you to prepare for those exams and tests.

93
00:06:38,040 --> 00:06:42,840
And there, of course, it's explained in all the detail.

94
00:06:43,810 --> 00:06:49,120
But you have to be ready for more complicated English.

95
00:06:50,620 --> 00:06:58,810
So this is not sometimes about understanding the things that are written, but also understanding the

96
00:06:58,810 --> 00:07:02,200
language, those more complicated structures.

97
00:07:02,530 --> 00:07:04,900
So if you are interested here with the link.

98
00:07:06,300 --> 00:07:07,080
All right.

99
00:07:07,080 --> 00:07:08,430
And we move on.

100
00:07:09,940 --> 00:07:15,400
As a specialist, you have to know what those things are.

101
00:07:15,430 --> 00:07:16,870
These are the basics.

102
00:07:16,870 --> 00:07:19,090
So of course, bug.

103
00:07:19,510 --> 00:07:25,060
Bug is a flaw in software that prevents it from performing its function.

104
00:07:25,120 --> 00:07:30,160
So yeah, I think this is also a definition from that glossary I mentioned.

105
00:07:30,490 --> 00:07:37,220
In other words, just the problem which prevents software from working well.

106
00:07:37,240 --> 00:07:37,780
Right.

107
00:07:37,780 --> 00:07:40,480
Because of this, the software doesn't work.

108
00:07:41,530 --> 00:07:49,450
As a QA specialist, you have to look for those bugs and you can say that you locate bugs.

109
00:07:49,450 --> 00:07:53,770
So finding them means locating them.

110
00:07:53,770 --> 00:07:55,730
So I locate bugs.

111
00:07:55,750 --> 00:08:04,560
It was difficult for me to locate that bug, but finally, I did it. Then debug.

112
00:08:04,570 --> 00:08:08,340
Well, that's actually something that a developer would do,

113
00:08:08,350 --> 00:08:09,640
right, debug.

114
00:08:10,150 --> 00:08:12,190
So fix those bugs.

115
00:08:13,210 --> 00:08:19,300
Then we have, of course, a bug report and you can say to create a bug report.

116
00:08:19,390 --> 00:08:29,200
So the list of those problems and related detail. And of course, a test case - a set of actions and instructions

117
00:08:29,200 --> 00:08:33,280
for a tester to check a certain aspect of the software.

118
00:08:33,610 --> 00:08:38,830
So yeah, those steps you have to follow to check some part of a software.

119
00:08:39,970 --> 00:08:40,659
All right.

120
00:08:40,659 --> 00:08:45,070
So a few more terms here and we move on.

121
00:08:46,720 --> 00:08:52,960
So there are different, of course, aspects of that.

122
00:08:53,900 --> 00:08:55,820
Quality assurance, right.

123
00:08:55,970 --> 00:09:02,390
And today you probably heard a lot of people talk about usability.

124
00:09:02,840 --> 00:09:04,670
So what's usability?

125
00:09:06,080 --> 00:09:06,740
You probably know.

126
00:09:06,740 --> 00:09:08,690
But still, we'll look at this definition.

127
00:09:08,720 --> 00:09:15,590
Usability testing is used to check if the user interface is easy to use and understand.

128
00:09:16,460 --> 00:09:19,430
This is not the kind of testing that can be automated.

129
00:09:19,430 --> 00:09:21,290
So you need humans for that.

130
00:09:22,130 --> 00:09:28,070
And when we talk about that usability testing, we usually talk about

131
00:09:29,360 --> 00:09:34,880
UX, UI, GUI, those abbreviations, right.

132
00:09:35,780 --> 00:09:36,770
What do they mean?

133
00:09:36,800 --> 00:09:37,730
Let's look.

134
00:09:39,100 --> 00:09:43,230
So UX stands for user experience.

135
00:09:43,240 --> 00:09:48,400
Overall impression person gets from using a website or an application.

136
00:09:49,060 --> 00:09:49,630
Right.

137
00:09:49,630 --> 00:09:51,550
So how good is it?

138
00:09:51,560 --> 00:09:53,020
Does it work well?

139
00:09:53,050 --> 00:09:56,440
Is it easy to use, easy to understand, things like that?

140
00:09:56,620 --> 00:10:01,390
Well, and UI is a part of that whole user experience.

141
00:10:01,390 --> 00:10:04,930
UI stands for user interface, right.

142
00:10:05,410 --> 00:10:12,370
User interface or components of the software that allow a user to interact with it.

143
00:10:13,900 --> 00:10:16,630
So how can you open those things?

144
00:10:16,630 --> 00:10:18,250
How can you close them?

145
00:10:18,250 --> 00:10:25,810
How to I don't know, write something there or give it a like, things like that.

146
00:10:26,170 --> 00:10:28,930
What can you do with that app and how it can do that?

147
00:10:29,530 --> 00:10:38,200
And the part of that UI is GUI, probably you've heard this abbreviation, GUI, graphic user interface.

148
00:10:39,220 --> 00:10:44,860
This is not only one because there is also a voice user interface, for example, but today we'll talk

149
00:10:44,860 --> 00:10:52,210
about graphic user interface, a type of interface that allows users to interact with the component

150
00:10:52,210 --> 00:10:58,000
or systems for graphical icons and visual indicators.

151
00:10:58,210 --> 00:11:04,690
So yeah, all those buttons, all those icons, they are

152
00:11:05,690 --> 00:11:09,530
elements of graphic user interface.

153
00:11:11,310 --> 00:11:19,650
And in the next part of this lecture, we are going to talk about those components.

154
00:11:19,650 --> 00:11:27,210
About those elements, because if you work in QA, it's important for you to know how they are called. 

